F212 — a read-only entry can call a procedure and mutate state

qpi.h states the rule in the C++ type system and clang enforces it there: a function takes
`const QpiContextFunctionCall&`, a procedure takes `const QpiContextProcedureCall&`, and
`QpiContextProcedureCall : public QpiContextFunctionCall`. `CALL(p, in, out)` forwards the caller's
own context, so a function calling a procedure passes a base where a derived is required:

    no viable conversion from 'const QPI::QpiContextFunctionCall' to 'const QPI::QpiContextProcedureCall'

The scaffold rewrites `CALL(f, in, out)` to `__qpi_call_self(f, in, out)`, which moves the target out
of callee position into an argument — and with it the callee's declared parameter types, and the
conversion. So the contract compiled and ran, and `state.mut().counter += 1` executed inside a query.

Adds a contract-level check alongside validateLogCalls: the caller's context class must be the
callee's or derive from it, resolved through the declared base chain. It therefore allows what C++
allows — a procedure calling a function is derived-to-base — and covers the proposal contexts without
naming them. Transitivity is free: an intermediate private function is itself a function, so it is
flagged at its own call site.

Measured: both backends now refuse ReadOnlyFunctionCallsPrivateProcedure. Suite 1085/0.

